n a year.\n\nBullion rose slightly to trade just below $3,900 an ounce. Prices briefly hit a 13-month low on Thursday as investors ramped up bets on the Federal Reserve raising interest rates. Yields on some Treasuries surged by more than 12 basis points in the previous session, with the biggest increases seen in short-term securities that are more sensitive to changes in central bank policy.\n\nThe decline \u201cat the end of the New York session was concerning but correlated well with the rising bond yields,\u201d said Rajeev De Mello, global macro portfolio manager at GAMA Asset Managem", "citation": "Gold Edges Up, Still On Track for Worst Weekly Slump Since June \u2014 Mint Markets, 2026-11-13. https://www.livemint.com/market/gold-edges-up-still-on-track-for-worst-weekly-slump-since-june-11794595223804.html"}, {"title": "Gem, jewellery exports up 34% on growing demand, rise in gold prices", "feed": "BusinessLine Mkts", "ts": "2026-11-13T10:49:20+00:00", "url": "https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/markets/gold/gem-jewellery-exports-up-34-on-growing-demand-rise-in-gold-prices/article71697017.ece", "status": "ok", "excerpt": "In rupee terms, it was up 45 per cent to \u20b927,863 crore (\u20b919,254 crore), according to the data released by the Gem and Jewellery Export Promotion Council data released on Friday. | Photo Credit: REUTERS/FRANCIS MASCARENHAS\n\nGem and jewellery exports increased 34 per cent last month to $2.92 billion against $2.18 logged in the same period last year.\n\nIn rupee terms, it was up 45 per cent to \u20b927,863 crore (\u20b919,254 crore), according to the data released by the Gem and Jewellery Export Promotion Council data released on Friday.\n\nThe strong October performance reflects the industry\u2019s continued export momentum and its efforts to meet international buyer requirements ahead of the year-end holiday se", "citation": "Gem, jewellery exports up 34% on growing demand, rise in gold prices \u2014 BusinessLine Mkts, 2026-11-13.